# mail — real-world examples

> Command-line email program — install `mailutils` (Debian) or `mailx` (Tumbleweed: `sudo zypper install mailx`).

## Send a subject-only message

```sh
mail -s "Test Subject" user@domain.com < /dev/null
```

## Send an attachment

```sh
mail -a /home/user/Documents/file.1 -s "file sent" user@domain.com < /dev/null
```

## Send to multiple recipients

```sh
mail -a /home/user/Documents/file.1 -s "file sent" user@domain.com,user2@domain.com < /dev/null
```

## Pipe a body in with escapes interpreted

```sh
MAIL_TO="username@example.com distrolist@example.com"
echo -e "Hello, \n\nService httpd is down on $HOST" | \
  mail -s "Webserver service down on $HOST" $MAIL_TO
```

## Write a message interactively

```
mail user@domain.com
Subject: <type subject>
Body of message
Body of message
Ctrl+D    # send (Ctrl+C twice aborts)
```

## Homelab: alert yourself when a long job finishes on homelab

```sh
zypper dup -y && echo "dup finished OK on $(hostname)" | \
  mail -s "homelab: zypper dup done" bwdave@protonmail.com
```

## Homelab: nightly disk report from mediaserver via cron

```sh
# /etc/cron.d/disk-report
0 7 * * * david df -h /srv/plex | mail -s "mediaserver disk report" bwdave@protonmail.com
```

| Flag | What it does              |
| ---- | ------------------------- |
| -s   | subject                   |
| -a   | attach a file             |
| -c   | CC address                |
| -b   | BCC address               |
